    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, Section 1.1.1",
      "anchor": "The constant-coefficient linear acoustics equations (1.5) can be viewed as conservation laws for pressure and velocity.",
      "observation": "This is the selected explicit mathematical claim. The following sentence separately qualifies the physical conservation interpretation."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, paragraph discussing acoustics",
      "anchor": "Physically, however, these are not conserved quantities except approximately in the case of very small amplitude disturbances in uniform media.",
      "observation": "Physical conservation of pressure and velocity is approximate and restricted to the stated regime."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2, equation (1.5) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "p_t(x,t) + K u_x(x,t) = 0; u_t(x,t) + (1/ρ) p_x(x,t) = 0",
      "observation": "The acoustic system has pressure and particle velocity as its two unknown functions; K and ρ are constant material parameters."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2, paragraph following (1.5)",
      "anchor": "pressure (or more properly the perturbation from some background constant pressure)",
      "observation": "p is more precisely a pressure perturbation, u is particle velocity, K is the bulk modulus of compressibility, and ρ is density."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2, equation (1.6)",
      "anchor": "q = [p; u], A = [[0, K], [1/ρ, 0]]",
      "observation": "The component order is pressure followed by velocity, and the acoustic system is q_t + A q_x = 0 with this constant matrix."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1, equation (1.1) and surrounding text",
      "anchor": "q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m",
      "observation": "The inherited setting is one spatial dimension with real space and time variables, a real vector of unknown functions, a constant real matrix, and subscripts denoting partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3, Section 1.1, equations (1.8)–(1.9); continuing on raw PDF page 26, printed page 4",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0; linear flux function f(q) = Aq",
      "observation": "The source defines conservation-law differential form and explicitly identifies constant-coefficient linear systems as conservation laws with flux Aq."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, Section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral conservation form is d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)); change in the interval total is determined by endpoint fluxes."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, Section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "The source conditions derivation of differential form from integral form on sufficient smoothness."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "Real spatial variable x and time variable t; the inherited state function has domain ℝ × ℝ [printed p. 1, (1.1), 'q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m'].",
      "Real-valued pressure perturbation p(x,t) and particle velocity u(x,t), forming q(x,t) = (p(x,t),u(x,t)) in ℝ² [printed p. 2, (1.5)–(1.6)].",
      "Constant material parameters K, the bulk modulus of compressibility, and ρ, the density [printed p. 2, paragraph following (1.5)].",
      "For the surrounding integral interpretation, any two spatial endpoints x₁ and x₂ [printed p. 4, (1.10), 'for any two points']." 
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "p and u satisfy the constant-coefficient linear acoustic equations p_t + K u_x = 0 and u_t + (1/ρ)p_x = 0 [printed p. 2, (1.5); printed p. 4, selected acoustics sentence].",
      "K and ρ are independent of space and time, as material constants in the selected constant-coefficient system [printed p. 2, paragraph following (1.5); printed p. 4, 'constant-coefficient'].",
      "The differential expressions require the relevant partial derivatives; sufficient smoothness is explicitly required in the surrounding discussion when deriving differential form from integral form, without a specified function class [printed p. 1, derivative convention following (1.1); printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2]."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The constant-coefficient linear acoustic equations are conservation laws for pressure and velocity [printed p. 4, selected sentence in Section 1.1.1].",
      "Specializing the explicitly stated linear flux f(q) = Aq to the acoustic state and matrix gives f(p,u) = (Ku,p/ρ); consequently their differential conservation form is p_t + ∂_x(Ku) = 0 and u_t + ∂_x(p/ρ) = 0. This explicit component flux is an algebraic inference from the source formulas [printed p. 2, (1.6); printed pp. 3–4, (1.8) and 'linear flux function f(q) = Aq'].",
      "The mathematical conservation-law description does not assert exact physical conservation of pressure and velocity: the source gives only an approximate physical interpretation for very small amplitude disturbances in uniform media [printed p. 4, sentence beginning 'Physically, however']." 
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "The state order is q = (p,u), with matrix A having rows (0,K) and (1/ρ,0) [printed p. 2, (1.6)].",
      "p denotes pressure, more properly its perturbation from a constant background pressure; u denotes particle velocity [printed p. 2, paragraph following (1.5)].",
      "Subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives with respect to time and space [printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1)].",
      "Differential conservation-law form means q_t + ∂_x(f(q)) = 0, where f is the flux function [printed p. 3, (1.8)].",
      "The surrounding integral convention uses left endpoint flux minus right endpoint flux: d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)) [printed p. 4, (1.10)]."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The setting is a first-order system in one space dimension; the acoustic system models waves propagating in both directions along a one-dimensional medium [printed p. 1, introduction to (1.1); printed p. 2, introduction to (1.5)].",
      "The selected assertion applies the preceding general observation that a constant-coefficient linear system is a conservation law with flux Aq [printed pp. 3–4, sentence spanning the page break after (1.9)].",
      "The very small amplitude and uniform-medium qualification concerns physical conservation, as signaled by 'Physically, however'; it is not separately stated as a hypothesis needed for the algebraic flux rewriting [printed p. 4, selected paragraph]."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Exact mathematical conservation form",
        "details": [
          "The two conserved state components in the formal PDE description are pressure and velocity themselves, in that order [printed p. 4, selected sentence; printed p. 2, (1.6)].",
          "Their associated component fluxes are Ku and p/ρ, inferred from the acoustic matrix and the prescribed flux Aq [printed p. 2, (1.6); printed p. 4, opening sentence].",
          "Constancy of the coefficients permits moving K and 1/ρ inside spatial derivatives; no variable-coefficient version is asserted here [printed p. 4, 'constant-coefficient linear acoustics equations'; printed p. 2, (1.5)]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Integral interpretation",
        "details": [
          "The surrounding conservation-law interpretation describes interval totals changing through endpoint fluxes, not unconditional constancy of those totals [printed p. 4, (1.10) and its explanatory paragraph].",
          "Specialization of (1.10) gives endpoint terms K u(x₁,t) − K u(x₂,t) for pressure and p(x₁,t)/ρ − p(x₂,t)/ρ for velocity; this is an inferred specialization, not a separately displayed acoustic equation [printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 2, (1.6)]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Physical limitation",
        "details": [
          "Approximate physical conservation is restricted to very small amplitude disturbances in uniform media [printed p. 4, 'Physically, however'].",
          "The following discussion distinguishes this acoustic model from the Euler equations, which model conservation of mass, momentum, and energy [printed p. 4, acoustics paragraph, 'These equations model the conservation of mass, momentum, and energy']." 
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve both acoustic equations and both state components; the selected claim concerns pressure and velocity together [printed p. 2, (1.5)–(1.6); printed p. 4, selected sentence].",
    "Preserve the coefficients: pressure flux Ku and velocity flux p/ρ follow from the specified matrix and flux Aq [printed p. 2, (1.6); printed p. 4, opening sentence].",
    "Preserve the constant-coefficient scope [printed p. 4, 'constant-coefficient linear acoustics equations (1.5)'].",
    "Do not turn the approximate physical interpretation into exact physical conservation [printed p. 4, 'not conserved quantities except approximately'].",
    "Do not assert interval totals are constant without accounting for endpoint fluxes [printed p. 4, (1.10)].",
    "Do not attribute an unconditional equivalence between integral and differential formulations to this passage: its discussion expressly invokes sufficient smoothness [printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2]."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "No explicit numerical domain restrictions on K and ρ are stated in the selected claim.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 2 identifies K as bulk modulus and ρ as density, uses 1/ρ in (1.5)–(1.6), and later writes c = √(K/ρ), but the selected sentence on printed p. 4 supplies no inequalities.",
      "impact": "The ordinary reciprocal requires nonzero ρ, and the physical terminology suggests positive material parameters, but explicit positivity hypotheses should not be reported as stated in this passage."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The precise regularity class is unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, says 'sufficiently smooth' without naming a differentiability or integrability class.",
